c-yards spot.</p><p><strong>George Kittle</strong> has room to work \u2014 LA allow <strong>41 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Christian McCaffrey</strong> eats as a receiver too \u2014 LA give up 23 rec yds/game to backs, and SF target the RB on 25% of throws.</p><p><strong>Puka Nacua</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 SF hold WR1s to just 51 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Kyren Williams</strong> is a bell-cow (68% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.67 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Colby Parkinson</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 SF are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.31 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Mike Evans</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 LA surrender 0.59 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>LA \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>LA are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (44% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Kyren Williams</strong> (13 carries, 58 yards) projects to his baseline workload \u2014 no strong script push either way in a close game.</p><p><strong>Puka Nacua</strong> projects for <strong>10 targets and 93 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Colby Parkinson</strong> (TE, 5 targets, 35 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>SF \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>SF are one of the league's m